00:03:35.705 --> 00:03:40.630
So let's start with domain
integration scenarios.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.110
So first of all is a new
customer provisioning.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:46.480
Where it starts is,
you have a customer

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.880
who is ready with a domain
and who wants to buy G Suite.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.350
What ends up in this floor
is-- you create a customer in G

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:55.870
Suite, you decide the
right G Suite subscription

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.020
to buy, get the
right payment plan,

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.590
buy it, create the
initial administrators,

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:05.290
set them in a working state,
verify the domain when you can,

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:08.550
and keep the customer in a
state that the admins, they all

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.050
can just log in to
Google for the first time

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:15.945
and get their email and
everything working immediately.

00:04:15.945 --> 00:04:19.610
The next one is for the existing
customers the general lifecycle

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:20.519
even changes.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:23.130
Customers might want to upgrade
or downgrade their G Suite

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.010
subscriptions, buy
add-ons, or there

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.350
might be suspended or
activated plan changes-- a lot

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:32.190
of activity for
existing customers.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.670
Then the most important
point is understanding bills.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.130
So two parts for this--

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.230
first part is
understanding how Google

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:43.260
bills for each and every
customer and subscription.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:47.040
So partners are interested in,
for every customer subscription

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.790
created, when does
Google start billing,

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.430
how much does Google bill
for its subscription,

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:56.780
and many times when would this
billing for a subscription

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.392
pause and when it would resume.

00:04:58.392 --> 00:05:00.350
And once all of that
information is there-- how

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.420
to, in the right way, bill
the customer appropriately

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.960
for all these scenarios.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.340
Then another very
important thing

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:12.300
is how to react to most of the
unexpected events that happen.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.840
So not every kind of activity
that happens on your customer

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.600
are actually initiated
by our partners.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:22.110
Many times our
subscription could be

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.810
suspended by Google for abuse.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.780
A new customer might,
when they signed the TOS

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.210
for the first time, their
subscription gets activated.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.601
This is not in partner's
control when it happens.

00:05:32.601 --> 00:05:35.100
And a lot of other activities
like on flexible subscriptions

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:39.450
when customers actually add
more users or delete users--

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:42.930
all of them have important
repercussions for partners

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.010
and how to deal with them.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:46.930
And the end is whenever
a customer decides

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.320
to close their account or they
transfer out of their partner,

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:55.000
how to actually gracefully
handle those cases.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.200
So let's go to the API
integrations, which

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.410
are the most important points
for all kind of integrations.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:06.200
So this page goes into exactly
the primary APIs, which we will

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.660
always end up integrating with.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:12.380
The first one in blue is
obviously the reseller API

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:14.000
as part of Admin SDK.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.880
This is the primary API
for managing customers

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:18.810
and subscriptions.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.950
The next in green is the
Site Verification API.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:25.310
The primary purpose of this
is to verify our domain

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.360
which a customer owns.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:32.440
The red one is the
Directory API in Admin SDK.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.500
Primary use for this API is
to create and delete users

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.480
on your customers' accounts.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.620
And then the one in gray is
the new push notifications

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.950
which we launched, and they
work via the Cloud Pub/Sub APIs.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.560
We'll go over those in details.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:48.850
And then there are more APIs--

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.340
the Reporting API,
which you can use to get

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:53.920
aggregated detail
usage statistics

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:58.400
for all customers, and then
the License Manager API,

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.080
which will allow you to manage
in detail licenses for Vault

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:04.150
storage and a lot
of other things,

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:06.520
and then Directory
API, which includes

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.060
every aspect of managing your
customers, users, devices,

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:14.200
groups, everything.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.240
So push notifications--
these are very newly

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.600
launched notifications
by our team.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:23.080
And it's available for
all G Suite partners.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:25.270
The notifications are
delivered to partners

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:27.310
via the Cloud Pub/Sub API.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:30.550
And these notifications
are actually

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.940
near-instant, real-time
events about your customers

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:35.320
and subscriptions.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.490
Whenever something
interesting happens,

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:40.530
you'll get events for
all these scenarios.

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:44.710
We'll in later slides, go
over in detail how these work.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:47.430
So this is how you can actually
set up and get your push

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:48.750
notifications started.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.820
This is a one-time
step partners need

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.930
to do once in their lifetime.

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.410
And starting off
with the yellow boxes

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:58.730
indicate what you need to do
on the Cloud Pub/Sub APIs.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.410
You first enable the project
in your cloud console.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.450
You create service
accounts, which you'll

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.640
use for getting notifications.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.280
Then coming to the blue box,
this is the Reseller API.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.780
You call the Reseller API once
to register for notifications.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:15.490
We'll give you back a topic
name, which you can use again

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:19.740
to listen or start a
subscription on the Cloud

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:23.205
Pub/Sub API and receive.

00:08:23.205 --> 00:08:26.370
Listener endpoints-- you
can create push or pull

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:27.750
notifications.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:31.560
Both of them work well--
whichever suits you better.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.890
And this is-- again,
our online documentation

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:37.120
explains all of the details
on how to get it started

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.460
and this is the one time effort.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:44.316
Now, let's go into the
testing best practices.

00:08:44.316 --> 00:08:46.940
One of the primary challenges to
integrating with these flows--

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.850
you always have challenges
integrating with all APIs,

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:51.800
but what is so special
about these APIs?

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:55.400
So the first thing is that
these G Suite Reseller APIs,

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:58.220
you don't freely get
access to these APIs.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.770
You only get one
account per partner,

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:03.770
and that one is your
production account.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:05.870
And the most
important part of this

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:10.610
is every API call you make
has a cost implication.

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:13.730
So every subscription you
create, every order you make--

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:16.430
they will end up
actually getting

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.830
billed for those activities.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:22.400
And if you're working with
annual plans, whenever

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.260
you do an activity
or an order, you're

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:28.190
actually liable to pay the
bill for the entire year.

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:32.270
So it's not very easy to just
play around with these APIs

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:33.350
while developing.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:35.160
And the last part
is-- because you

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:40.940
have one account, it's
development activity, which

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:43.970
could have a bug or
inadvertently affect

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:45.920
your production
environment, because there's

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.080
one account all your
customers are on it.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:51.580
So there's a risk
integrating with these APIs.

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:53.750
What our partners
done in the past

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:57.620
before we had the
testing support was they

00:09:57.620 --> 00:09:59.940
used to test never
nevertheless on the production

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.300
account, the single
account which they had.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:04.340
And they had an
arrangement with Google

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:07.520
where they would do some
testing for some test domains

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.500
and then send Google the bill.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.210
And Google would
manually refund them.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:13.670
It was a painful
process, but it worked.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:15.320
That process, as
I said, was always

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.650
susceptible to accidental
bugs, which would actually

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:21.810
harm your existing customers.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.820
The other option was to use
it old Reseller API Sandbox

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.380
environment for testing.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:29.600
It did not work
with any other APIs.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.310
And it was only working with
the Reseller APIs especially.

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:38.060
And there was no bills
involved with that.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:41.270
But really because it did
not work with other APIs,

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:44.322
you could not actually
test end-to-end with it.

00:10:44.322 --> 00:10:46.280
And not surprisingly,
because of those reasons,

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:48.170
no partner actually
found it useful.

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:51.710
And eventually we
just shut it down.

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:54.170
So apart from all
this, what still

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:56.210
could be the worse
that could happen

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:59.200
if you do not integrate well?

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:02.385
So just a question--
how many dollars

00:11:02.385 --> 00:11:07.050
do you think an accidental order
can cause due to some coding

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.540
bugs on outside?

00:11:12.067 --> 00:11:13.400
Anybody wants to take any guess?

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:19.050
Is it like $100,000,
$500,000, or more?

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:21.570
So half a million?

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.980
So yeah, the right
answer is $3 million.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:28.600
So this happened
not very long ago.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:33.840
Suddenly in our system, as
you've seen probably in movies,

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.060
you have some
back-end team running.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:39.960
And suddenly they start popping
up some severe exceptions.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:43.730
And some engineer is paged
about some special activity

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:44.230
happening.

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:48.420
This happened, and suddenly we
were called for the details.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:51.810
And we saw a partner
submitting an amazing amount

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:52.620
of orders for us.

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:54.210
Initially, we became very happy.

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:56.790
Some partner is really
getting big orders for us,

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:00.210
but looking deeply we
said something is wrong.

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:02.820
And we saw that within
a span of two days,

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.730
they had submitted
orders worth $3 million.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.964
And we got back to them,
and they looked at it.

00:12:08.964 --> 00:12:10.380
And after some
time, they realized

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:14.400
that an engineer made a coding
bug on their live system,

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.070
and all the orders and
everything coming to us

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.770
was starting to be
in such huge numbers

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:23.160
that we got up to $3
million of orders.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:26.460
So obviously, after this,
Google refunded them,

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:29.040
but the whole process
is just so painful.

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:30.780
Nobody wants to get to it.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:35.430
And believe me-- the importance
of revenue recognition,

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.890
bookkeeping, how
refunds, billing,

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.072
and everything is made--

00:12:39.072 --> 00:12:40.530
on our side also,
also, we realized

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:43.200
how complicated these things
are when we face these issues.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.600
So nobody at Google and
our partners-- nobody

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:49.625
wants to get into
such situations.

00:12:49.625 --> 00:12:51.990
So the solution we
recommend right now

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:55.040
is the Test Reseller Console.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:56.940
So basically, all
the active resellers

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:00.020
who have a reseller account
are eligible for getting

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:01.850
these accounts.

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:04.270
The way it works is
you create a test trial

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:07.670
G Suite account direct, and
then you contact Google.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:12.670
And we will convert that account
into a Test Reseller Console.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:14.654
The domain name
for those accounts

00:13:14.654 --> 00:13:16.820
need to follow some testing
conventions to make sure

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:20.790
that there are no abuses and no
overlaps with actual real world

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:23.380
customers.

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:25.270
How do these accounts work?

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:27.250
First, they're
pretty much exactly

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:29.740
like your regular accounts.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:32.080
The only exception is that
for all your customers

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:36.400
you need to keep following the
domain name guidelines, which

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:39.400
make sure that you don't
use real world domains.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:41.200
And then this account
is never billed.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:44.530
All the prices for all the
orders you do on this account

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:45.747
are zero.

00:13:45.747 --> 00:13:47.830
And this data, because
this is a separate account,

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:51.050
is totally separate from your
actual real world customers.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:53.170
So there is no risk
to actually harming

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.930
your production environment.

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:59.320
And you don't need to really
clean up whatever activity you

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:00.640
do in these accounts.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.220
Google will automatically
delete all the customers

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:05.930
created on test accounts
within a few days.

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:07.840
And we really don't
have any number

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:09.250
of limits of customers created.

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:12.090
So while testing,
there is nothing

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:13.090
you need to worry about.

00:14:15.695 --> 00:14:19.950
So I'll go over mainly the
brief testing practices

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:22.820
as we would recommend.

00:14:22.820 --> 00:14:26.420
So first of all is while your
initial development for G Suite

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:28.550
integrations, use these
accounts while you're

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:30.350
building your code
for the first time

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:33.110
and getting your
integrations ready.

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:35.460
Next is all the
individual developers.

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:39.140
They could be given a
credential on their account

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:41.100
for their regular activity.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.740
So whenever they
are working on bugs

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:44.490
or writing incremental
things, they

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:46.550
have their account handy
where they can actually

00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:50.240
make calls to Google and make
sure their code is working.

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:51.950
The third is whenever
you are actually

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.792
doing a live system rollout--

00:14:53.792 --> 00:14:56.000
maybe for the first time or
every incremental feature

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:56.810
with Google--

00:14:56.810 --> 00:15:00.457
do an end-to-end integration
testing of your entire scenario

00:15:00.457 --> 00:15:02.540
using these accounts to
make sure everything works

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:05.060
and end-to-end.

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:08.960
And at the end,
this how we set up

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:13.740
regular, weekly or daily
tests running automatically

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:18.030
using the test accounts
on your system.

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:20.610
This is the most important
thing I can tell you

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:25.710
from my experience I've seen in
the engineering in both Google

00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:27.070
and Microsoft for a long time.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:28.740
This comes really handy.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:31.560
Even with the best engineering
teams and everything,

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:35.510
if you have systems which
continuously keep testing,

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:37.950
you will always find out
issues before they actually

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:40.260
hit your live
production environment.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:42.510
This practice has been
a lifesaver generally

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:46.210
for all big companies,
and this is a no-brainer.

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:48.210
You always need to
have a regular test

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:50.590
activity happening.

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:53.140
So how many people
use this right now?

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:56.650
We have been giving this
out for one year right now.

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:58.300
All the new API partners get it.

00:16:00.892 --> 00:16:03.100
Even existing partners are
eligible to get an account

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:04.180
from Google.

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:07.060
We have over 30 partners
right now actively using it.

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:09.970
We have seen more than
10,000 customers created.

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:13.300
And as of now we see hundreds
of accounts being created daily.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:15.850
So clearly all
the partners using

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:18.225
are finding it very useful.

00:16:18.225 --> 00:16:20.670
So let's go into the more
detailed technical flows

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:23.540
right now.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:25.960
First of all, to
understand the data model,

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:29.110
the G Suite system has
two primary entities.

00:16:29.110 --> 00:16:30.940
The first one is the customer.

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:36.400
The customer has a
unique ID, which clearly

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:39.110
represents the customer.

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:42.160
The other thing customer
has is the primary domain.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:47.110
One domain can be the primary
domain which the customer owns.

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:50.170
And then they can have multiple
alias or secondary domains.

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:55.310
They can have a lot many
users in that domain.

00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:58.030
The second most important
entity are subscriptions.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:00.630
A single customer can have
multiple subscriptions.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:02.570
However, for a G
Suite, you can only

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:05.060
have one G Suite subscription.

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:06.680
You can also buy
add-on subscriptions

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:08.760
like Vault and Drive.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:15.119
Subscriptions consists of SKU
ID, a unique subscription ID.

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:17.480
They can have plan
details, number of seats,

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:21.020
renewals settings, et cetera.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:22.520
So let's go over
the detailed flows.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:25.234
What goes through in a
new customer provisioning?

00:17:25.234 --> 00:17:29.000
This shows what all integrations
a partner needs to do

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:32.990
for creating new customers.

00:17:32.990 --> 00:17:35.090
The first boxes
in green indicate

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:38.270
how you integrate with the
site verification APIs.

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:39.542
Whenever you get a domain--

00:17:39.542 --> 00:17:41.000
a brand new domain
which a customer

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.400
wants to sign up G Suite with--

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:45.860
you can call Google's
Site Verification

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:50.150
APIs to verify that the customer
really owns this domain.

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:51.860
This is very important
since once you

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:55.360
verify that the customer
really owns the domain,

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:58.000
Google services can
work seamlessly.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.310
The next blue
boxes are the calls

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:03.930
you make to Resellers APIs
for creating a customer.

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:07.510
The red boxes indicate
the Admin SDK APIs.

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:10.930
You have to create an
admin user, create a user

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:12.040
and make it an admin.

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:14.770
And at the end you
create a subscription.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:16.690
Once you've done all
this you're ready to have

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:19.765
the customer in a ready state.

00:18:19.765 --> 00:18:23.080
Some of the key points to focus
while you're creating a user

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:26.920
is all G Suite customers
are supposed to have

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:28.550
at least one administrator.

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:32.140
So you must create at
least one user initially,

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:34.060
and that user must
be administrator.

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:39.130
This admin must log into Google
once and sign the Google TOS

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:42.454
before their email or
anything else starts working.

00:18:42.454 --> 00:18:44.120
Now, this is not in
control of a partner

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:48.370
but the customer has to first
log in and get that activated.

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:51.140
The other site is 409 errors.

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:55.340
So if a customer comes to you,
and they want to buy a domain,

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:59.530
but they have some users
already using Google's consumer

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:02.170
services like YouTube,
Google Analytics,

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:04.600
those are considered
conflict situations.

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:07.870
And whenever you try to create
a user which already exists

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:10.000
using the services,
we do not directly

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:12.130
boot out the other user.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:13.832
The important
distinguishing factor here

00:19:13.832 --> 00:19:15.790
is that if you have
already verified the domain

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:19.420
while creating the
user, we will allow

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:22.460
you to create that domain, and
we will evict the other user.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:24.190
Evict means we'll not
delete the account,

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:26.880
but we'll send appropriate
action item for that account

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:31.360
or get rollover into
the real Google domain.

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:33.110
Unless you have
verified the domain,

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:34.300
you cannot do that step.

00:19:37.015 --> 00:19:38.820
The other part is
transferring a customer.

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:41.980
Not always all customers you
get are brand new customers.

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:45.710
Some of them are existing
and with other resellers

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:46.710
or directly with Google.

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:48.120
So you need to transfer that.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:51.330
The way you do that is first you
check whether a customer exists

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:54.630
or not while doing a
Customer.Get () call.

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:57.710
Then you manually ask the
customer for a transfer token.

00:19:57.710 --> 00:19:59.930
This is like a password
which the customer generate.

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:02.580
And using that password, you
can do a list subscription

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:04.830
on the customer and
insert subscription

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.840
on each or the subscription
which customer has.

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:10.350
You have to make sure you
do the insert subscriptions

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:11.740
in a single batch request.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:13.156
And once you do
that, the customer

00:20:13.156 --> 00:20:15.245
will be transferred
on your site.

00:20:15.245 --> 00:20:17.870
Some of the key points to
remember for transfers is that

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:22.010
while transferring an
existing customer to yourself,

00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:24.350
you cannot make
changes to the SKUs.

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:27.668
You must transfer all
the SKUs as it is.

00:20:27.668 --> 00:20:30.290
And all the
subscriptions transfers

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:32.480
must happen in a
single batch request.

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:35.250
You need a transfer
token from the customer.

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:37.964
And when you transfer
to a customer,

00:20:37.964 --> 00:20:39.630
you will create a
brand new subscription

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:41.255
with brand new renewals
and everything.

00:20:41.255 --> 00:20:43.140
So you don't carry
over whatever the plan

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:46.589
was with the customer earlier.

00:20:46.589 --> 00:20:48.130
And one of the key
things to remember

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:50.210
is that when you are
transferring customers

00:20:50.210 --> 00:20:51.670
on multiple
subscriptions, you need

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:53.590
to make sure that
on your own account,

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:56.260
you're already
signed all the TOSes.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:59.590
Some of Google's additional
services like Drive storage,

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:02.050
they require the
partners to sign the TOS.

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:04.109
If you have not signed
the TOS, the transfers

00:21:04.109 --> 00:21:04.900
will actually fail.

00:21:08.110 --> 00:21:10.350
Now, just to see how
notifications work--

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:14.380
whenever you get a new customer,
whether it was an existing

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:16.300
customer with Google
earlier or not,

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:19.920
you always get a new
subscription we just created.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:21.630
The moment the
subscription is created,

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.970
our push notifications
API will immediately

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:26.590
send you a notification
via Cloud Pub/Sub API.

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:32.000
On the right, you'll
see in green the details

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.220
of the subscription changes.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:38.099
If you see the bold
new event_type,

00:21:38.099 --> 00:21:39.890
you'll see here a
NEW_SUBSCRIPTION_CREATED.

00:21:39.890 --> 00:21:43.110
This exactly tells you what
type of an event was created.

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:46.750
And then based on what type
of event it was, in the red

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:50.040
if you see, there'll be
more specific details,

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:52.140
which will exactly tell
why that even occurred.

00:21:55.795 --> 00:21:58.390
So testing checkpoint
here is that when

00:21:58.390 --> 00:22:00.250
you have unboarded
this whole floor,

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:03.080
how do you make sure you'll
do integration testing?

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:05.290
So the first step is you
have a test set up ready

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:09.050
where you have the Reseller
Console accounts all the TOSes

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:10.360
signed, Cloud Pub/Sub project--

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.070
everything in a working state.

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:15.340
Then you execute your scenario
of creating a customer

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:19.150
within your flows, whether it's
a website or any kind of flow.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:21.760
But that flow should actually
go through all the steps

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:25.840
like to get customer domain
verification, admin creation

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:26.922
subscription, et cetera.

00:22:26.922 --> 00:22:29.380
And then, at the end, once you
have gone through that flow,

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:32.560
make sure you
verify fields like,

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:35.200
you make sure the Customer.Get
() retrieves an actual

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:35.994
customer.

00:22:35.994 --> 00:22:38.410
Subscriptions.List () creates
the right subscription which

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:40.270
you created.

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:41.860
And that once a
customer is created,

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:44.920
you get notifications for
a new subscription created.

00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:48.820
And the other notification
will be subscription suspended

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:50.980
because the moment you
create a subscription,

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.740
it is suspended until the
customer signs that TOS.

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:56.602
So once you receive these
notifications in your floor,

00:22:56.602 --> 00:22:58.810
you are insured that all
the integrations with Google

00:22:58.810 --> 00:23:00.040
are working fine.

00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:02.095
And again, a
reminder is that once

00:23:02.095 --> 00:23:03.970
you've done that, you
don't need to clean up.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:06.520
Google will automatically
clean up the accounts.

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:09.820
Let's go to how customer life
cycle changes are happening.

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:13.060
Quickly going through how to
upgrade to a G Suite business

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:14.680
subscription--

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:17.940
what you need to do is just
call in Subscriptions.Insert ()

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:22.040
on the G Suite business SKU ID,
and automatically your G Suite

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:25.600
Basic will get upgraded
to do the subscription.

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:28.900
Similarly, downgrade works
exactly the same as upgrade.

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:30.700
You just do a
Subscriptions.Insert () on G

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:33.970
Suite Basic, and your
earlier subscription will get

00:23:33.970 --> 00:23:36.005
downgraded.

00:23:36.005 --> 00:23:38.030
Now, coming back to
notifications for this--

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:40.520
the moment you upgrade or
downgrade, again, you'll

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:44.660
get a notification from
Cloud Pub/Sub with event type

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:47.045
saying that subscription
was upgraded or downgraded.

00:23:49.825 --> 00:23:52.950
Suspending a customer--
you can suspend

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.670
customers for up to 60 days.

00:23:55.670 --> 00:23:57.620
The way you do is
on a customer you've

00:23:57.620 --> 00:23:59.630
got, call list
subscriptions and suspend

00:23:59.630 --> 00:24:03.330
all of the subscriptions,
or whichever one you want.

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:04.830
One thing to note
is that whenever

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:06.310
you suspend flexible
subscriptions,

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:09.240
Google will pause billing
the partner for it,

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:12.660
but for annual subscriptions,
they'll still be billed

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:16.485
and the renewal data is
not changed for them.

00:24:16.485 --> 00:24:19.830
Activating a customer back
works exactly as the same calls

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:22.877
Subscriptions.Activate
() for the subscription.

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:28.840
Notifications for
these activities

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:31.430
for both of these
suspended and activated--

00:24:31.430 --> 00:24:34.740
notifications are sent
with the appropriate type,

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:37.069
SUBSCRIPTION_SUSPENDED
or SUSPENSION_REVOKED.

00:24:37.069 --> 00:24:39.110
And you will also have an
additional reason field

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:42.340
which will give the reason as
RESELLER_INITIATED, indicating

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:44.620
that the suspension happened
because the partner had

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.390
initiated.

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:50.795
Some of the important points
to notice about suspension

00:24:50.795 --> 00:24:55.100
are that if you do not activate
the subscriptions after 60

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:59.180
days, Google might cancel them
or transfer them over directly

00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:01.080
to Google.

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:03.090
Suspensions could be
for multiple reasons--

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:05.190
if the customer
does not accept TOS

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:08.710
for abuse reasons, if
their trial period ended,

00:25:08.710 --> 00:25:09.840
or something.

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:12.840
But whenever you
call activate, you

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:16.232
can only remove the reason
with reseller initiated.

00:25:16.232 --> 00:25:18.190
That means you can only
activate subscriptions,

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:22.910
which were suspended
by the partner itself.

00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:24.970
I covered earlier
flexible subscriptions

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:29.410
are paused for billing and
annual subscriptions are not.

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:32.340
And one more thing is that new
customers that always suspended

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:35.310
by default, and the moment
they signed the TOS,

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:36.924
they are activated.

00:25:39.267 --> 00:25:41.100
Going through the testing
checkpoint, again,

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:43.470
similar to customer creation
for these activities--

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:47.940
how to set up ready with all the
things, how a customer created

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:49.410
and subscriptions ready.

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:52.840
Then run your scenario
in your integration flow,

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:55.720
which ends up calling
based on each situation,

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:58.080
Subscription.Insert
for either upgrade

00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:00.750
or downgrade or the suspension
or activation flows.

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:04.860
Once you do that, you should be
able to verify by making sure

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:06.840
you received the right events.

00:26:06.840 --> 00:26:08.580
Pertaining to each
of the activity,

00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:10.850
the right set of events must
be received from Google.

00:26:13.485 --> 00:26:17.530
Coming to the important
area of bills,

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:23.710
the main events, which actually
cause Google to bill partners,

00:26:23.710 --> 00:26:28.660
is primarily broken down into
where the subscription changes.

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:31.330
Whenever a new subscription
is created or the trial

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:34.930
subscription converted
to a paid billing starts,

00:26:34.930 --> 00:26:36.830
if a subscription is
suspended or activated,

00:26:36.830 --> 00:26:40.270
billing could pause or unpause
for flexible subscriptions.

00:26:40.270 --> 00:26:42.790
Whenever you cancel an
upgrade or downgrade,

00:26:42.790 --> 00:26:44.560
that will end up
changing the billing.

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:47.500
And then, coming to the
payment plan changes,

00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:49.590
whenever an annual commitment--

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:52.600
the number of committed seats
are increased or decreased.

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:56.980
Whenever the annual plan
renews, or in the flexible plan

00:26:56.980 --> 00:27:00.894
cases, whenever more seats
are added or some are removed.

00:27:00.894 --> 00:27:02.810
And whenever a customer
decides or the partner

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:05.900
decides to change payment
plan for the customer.

00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:08.270
And for all of these events,
the push notifications

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:09.560
are send to partners.

00:27:12.225 --> 00:27:14.220
One thing to note
for flexible billing

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:18.540
is that every day the number
of current licenses held

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:21.960
by the subscription
are used for billing.

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:24.840
So the way it works is when
you do a Subscriptions.List ()

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:27.570
and you get the subscriptions,
the field license number

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:31.470
of seats at that time tells you
how many seats for that day are

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:33.430
being charged by Google.

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:36.850
The right way to actually
keep track of all these things

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:40.600
is arguably to suggest partners
to run periodical cron jobs

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:43.840
and fetch this list to make
sure they know what exactly they

00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:44.766
are getting billed.

00:27:44.766 --> 00:27:46.890
But now, once you subscribe
to notification events,

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:48.806
you will get all the
right notification events

00:27:48.806 --> 00:27:52.310
whenever something changes
on a flexible subscription.

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:55.010
So you don't need to really
be calling Google repeatedly

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:56.890
for this.

00:27:56.890 --> 00:28:01.900
And some of the billable events
which a partner cannot control.

00:28:01.900 --> 00:28:04.330
The first one is, for a
flexible subscription,

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:07.120
if a customer goes
and accepts the TOS.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:10.000
Not all customers would
accept the TOS immediately

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:13.690
after buying, so they would
probably take a week or more.

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:17.200
So in those cases,
the moment they

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:18.670
signed the TOS,
Google will start

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:20.395
billing flexible subscriptions.

00:28:20.395 --> 00:28:22.720
Next is when admins
add or remove users

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:23.980
in the flexible plans.

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:27.340
Partners cannot control when the
customer administration can do

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:28.334
that.

00:28:28.334 --> 00:28:31.000
And then subscription-- whenever
they get suspended or activated

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:34.300
for other reasons not
in partners control.

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:36.460
And then whenever a
customer transfers away

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:40.450
from a partner without
partner initiating it.

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:43.092
So notifications are sent
for all of these cases.

00:28:43.092 --> 00:28:44.550
So if you listen
for notifications,

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:47.405
you should be able
to deal with all.

00:28:47.405 --> 00:28:49.170
Going to just testing
checkpoint again--

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:53.270
how to do all these things
are same set of startups--

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:56.090
get everything working,
have all customers

00:28:56.090 --> 00:28:57.740
in a flexible subscription.

00:28:57.740 --> 00:29:01.600
Then in the execute
session you could

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:05.930
try logging to the Admin Console
as a customer administrator

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:07.580
and accept the TOS.

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:09.290
And then go and try
to delete or add

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:12.320
users in customer's Admin
Console either by the API

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:13.370
or by the UI.

00:29:13.370 --> 00:29:16.220
But for each of these case, the
moment the administrator signs

00:29:16.220 --> 00:29:20.090
the TOS, you should get an event
that subscription suspension

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:20.990
was revoked.

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:23.940
And for the users
created or deleted,

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:26.240
you will get events like
LICENSE_ASSIGNMENT_CHANGED,

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:29.000
which will indicate that
something about the seat

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:30.560
activity is changed.

00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:33.260
And you should get the
new field immediately

00:29:33.260 --> 00:29:37.060
and see the right value.

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:40.110
Finally, coming to a
customer off-boarding--

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:42.600
so how do you cancel a customer?

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:46.080
Whenever a customer decides
to either close their account

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:48.270
or to cancel a relationship
with a reseller,

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:51.250
you would end up just
transferring the customer back

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:51.750
to Google.

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:53.292
So important thing
to remember is

00:29:53.292 --> 00:29:54.750
that Google does
not allow partners

00:29:54.750 --> 00:29:56.520
to delete customer's accounts.

00:29:56.520 --> 00:29:58.290
You can just transfer
them to Google.

00:29:58.290 --> 00:30:01.470
The way you do is do a list
subscriptions on the customer

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:04.760
and, for each subscription, do
a Subscriptions.Delete () with

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:06.660
the reason as
transfer_to_direct.

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:09.870
And make sure you do all
of these as a batch call.

00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:11.910
Once you do that, the
customer is gratefully

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:14.280
transferred to Google,
and you don't need

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:17.330
to worry about the customer.

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:19.470
The other cases
are when a customer

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:22.384
transfers outside a
partner without actually

00:30:22.384 --> 00:30:23.300
notifying the partner.

00:30:23.300 --> 00:30:25.080
This could happen if
the customer contacts

00:30:25.080 --> 00:30:27.200
Google support to
transfer, or a customer

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:29.400
gets transferred
to another partner,

00:30:29.400 --> 00:30:30.660
and they transfer away.

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:32.970
In these cases, as
in the blue box,

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:35.710
the customer just
initiates the transfer.

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:39.220
In the red box, the
transfer happens through,

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:41.620
and the partner
loses the customer.

00:30:41.620 --> 00:30:44.800
And how the partners
react in these cases is,

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:47.260
they need to settle
the final bills

00:30:47.260 --> 00:30:51.280
and expect Google to stop
billing them for this customer.

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:54.730
For these cases right now, we
are sending API notifications

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:56.722
using those push notifications.

00:30:56.722 --> 00:30:58.180
So listening to
the activities, you

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:00.760
can get the events whenever
a customer transfers out

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:02.270
immediately.

00:31:02.270 --> 00:31:04.960
We also send email
notifications in such cases.

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:11.102
So this is just talking about
all off-boarded customers.

00:31:11.102 --> 00:31:12.560
Whenever a customer
is off-boarded,

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:15.250
either by the partner
or for any reason,

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:16.900
you always get a
notification for that.

00:31:19.505 --> 00:31:21.766
Coming to testing checkpoint
again for this flow--

00:31:21.766 --> 00:31:23.140
in your integration
flow when you

00:31:23.140 --> 00:31:24.910
run through the
scenario of closing

00:31:24.910 --> 00:31:29.040
in customer's account, which
goes to the actual Google

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:30.670
events, which need
to be called, you

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:33.010
should verify it by
waiting for subscriptions

00:31:33.010 --> 00:31:38.420
and making sure the right
subscriptions are called back.

00:31:38.420 --> 00:31:42.460
And coming finally to support--

00:31:42.460 --> 00:31:44.200
if you have any
questions, in general,

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:46.390
Google's online documentation
has all the details

00:31:46.390 --> 00:31:49.030
about all these APIs
and other things.

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:52.420
If you need a Test
Reseller Console account,

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:54.190
you can open a support
ticket with Google,

00:31:54.190 --> 00:31:56.720
and we can help
you get an account.

00:31:56.720 --> 00:31:59.720
And for any API you choose
which you're facing,

00:31:59.720 --> 00:32:01.310
when you open a
support ticket, make

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:04.430
sure you get all the details
right, like the full header

00:32:04.430 --> 00:32:07.790
and response details,
authentication methods,

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:10.314
your reseller or customer
domain name, and all details

00:32:10.314 --> 00:32:12.230
so Google can appropriately
address this form.

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:17.350
So I'll be doing a
little bit to recap.

00:32:17.350 --> 00:32:20.090
The primary things are--

00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:23.480
so you can just contact Google
and get a Test Reseller Console

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:27.260
account, and follow all the
best practices, which I just

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:29.610
described.

00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:31.600
And remember about the
push notifications.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:34.080
Use these to cover
all the integration

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:37.020
holes in your scenarios.

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:40.730
And keep in mind all the corner
details, which we discussed,

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:43.360
which require special attention.

